# Each construction of a group's `record` that ends with `rest: zero` (panel
# 194; spec § 13), every field it does not name zero, marked for the emitter.
# The parser took the words off the argument list (`parse/rest_words.hero`),
# so the construction reaches `check/walk.hero` naming some of its fields,
# which is panel 186's partial construction: its labels are checked there
# (`check/group_fields.hero`), C zeroes every byte of the record before it
# writes the fields named (`emit/zeroed.hero`, which reads `rested`), and
# `build` stops asking whether a field left out shares a byte with one named
# (`emit/layout_sites.hero`). Two fields named in one union are still refused
# there.
#
# Which call the words may end is the name stage's question, and every other
# call ending with them is told there, at the words, beside the names
# (`resolve/rest_zero.hero`, defect 401): this module asks the same
# `reading` and marks what it calls `builds`, so the rule is written once. A
# handle is marked nowhere: its construction is refused whole by
# `check/handle_built.hero` (defect 397).
#
# A flat scan of the arena, run from `checker.hero` after the bodies, as
# `ffi_sweep` is: it reads what the resolver said each callee is, and no type.

use ast
use source
use resolved
use check/state
use resolve/rest_zero as rest_reading

function judged(@c: state.Checker, a: ast.Ast, r: resolved.Resolved, s: source.Source, decls: [ast.Decl])
    # Nothing to mark, and no walk, where no call ends with the words.
    if a.rests.len() == 0
        return
    at: i64 @ -1

    for node in a.exprs
        at @ at + 1
        ended = rest_reading.ended(a, r.uses, at)

        if ended.is_err()
            continue

        match rest_reading.reading(s, decls, ended.must())
            .builds            => c.out.rested[node.span.end] @ true
            .handle | .refused => continue
